The aluminum-magnesium alloys are non age-hardenable and are standardized in the 5000 series. The determined chemical composition by glow discharge optical emission spectroscopy (GDOES) of the aluminum alloy is given in Table  1 a). It corresponds to the standard DIN EN 573-3 [ 10 ] except for the tin content. However, the GDOES measurements exhibited an increased standard deviation (SD) for Sn of 0.084. Given the inherent uncertainty associated with this analytical technique, the observed minor deviation remains within acceptable limits, and the alloy may therefore be classified as commercially pure. The mechanical properties were determined on three specimens oriented along the rolling direction with quasi-static tensile tests according to DIN EN ISO 6892-1 [ 14 ]. The results in terms of elastic modulus E, ultimate tensile strength R m , uniform elongation A g , and the offset yield strength at 0.2 % plastic deformation R p0.2  are summarized in Table  2 a). The sheet material used for subsequent blanking experiments had a thickness of 2 mm. Table 1 Chemical composition (wt. %) according to GDOES measurements with standard deviation (SD) for  a ) EN AW-5754,  b ) zinc alloy,  c ) tin alloy,  d ) 22MnB5,  e ) 1.4301 and  f ) DC06 a) EN AW-5754 Si Fe Cu Mn Mg Cr Zn Sn Ti DIN EN 573-3 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
